00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.160
your games in a lot
of different ways

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.020
across many different channels.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.930
Today we're going to focus on
two of those logos, the second

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.190
and the third from the left.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:43.930
We're going to talk
about Google Cloud

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.903
for running your game servers
and your back end services.

00:01:46.903 --> 00:01:49.195
And we're also going to talk
a little bit about Stadia.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.120
So Google Cloud's
mission for games--

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.850
so as it turns out, building
and scaling a global multiplayer

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.590
game has quite a bit of
complexity involved in it.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.710
And if there's two things
that Google is really good at,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.229
it's scaling things
up really big--

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.270
we currently have
eight services that

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.370
have a billion users
in them each, daily.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.130
So Google really
focuses on scaling.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.100
And we also focus on
reducing complexity.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.060
The reason that services like
Gmail and YouTube and Docs

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.400
became so popular
is because we were

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.390
able to take the complexity
out of using those services.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.820
So we want to bring that
same sensibility to games.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.557
We want to scale
games up really big.

00:02:36.557 --> 00:02:39.140
And we want to make it as easy
as possible for you to do that.

00:02:42.207 --> 00:02:44.040
So we have four pillars
that we believe will

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.660
help us deliver on our mission.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.120
Number one, cross platform.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.110
Cross platform and cross play
have become really important

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.600
concepts recently with games.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.850
Players want to be
able to enjoy a game

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.730
on a device of their choosing.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.510
Game servers are a
critical component

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.980
in delivering cross platform
and cross play experiences.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.480
Google is a leader
in open source.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.740
So with Kubernetes, we
established a de facto

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.340
open standard for interacting
with infrastructure

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.720
in the Cloud.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.480
Kubernetes is your
open source API

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.990
to compute and storage
running in any cloud,

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.300
or in your own data centers.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.790
We want to bring that
same sensibility to games.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.360
So for any of the games
services that we're building,

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.850
we're going to go open source
because we believe you should

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.430
be able to run them anywhere.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.040
This is in stark contrast
to the gaming services

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.950
that you might find
in other clouds, which

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:52.440
tend to be proprietary, and
involve a degree of lock in.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.140
So Google also has a lot of
expertise in machine learning

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:55.980
and analytics.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.440
And we're very excited about
bringing our capabilities

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.563
to bear for games in
this space as well.

00:04:01.563 --> 00:04:03.480
And I think that first
slide that we looked at

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.330
does a really nice job of
discussing that One Google

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.220
story.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.590
We have a lot of different
ways to help build and publish

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.108
amazing games.

00:04:12.108 --> 00:04:14.400
And we're going to explore
One Google a little bit more

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.329
when we talk about GCP
and Stadia later on.

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:26.140
So what is our Cloud
for Games team building?

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.560
We're focusing on
three different areas.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.630
The first is game
server hosting.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.390
We're focused on making
our compute platform

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.820
the best and most
high performance place

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.740
to run your games.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.640
This is going to be the
focus of our talk today.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.720
But to run a large
multiplayer game,

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.150
you also need platform services.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.730
You need a place
to run your APIs,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.630
so that your users can log in
and get assigned to a match.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.650
So we're doing work there.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:57.490
And we're also bringing
the scale of our analytics

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:01.480
and machine learning
solutions to gaming as well.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.138
So we're going to focus mostly
on game servers for today.

00:05:04.138 --> 00:05:06.055
But we're working in all
three of those areas.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:12.280
So this is our reference
architecture for games.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:16.350
So most games have a
fairly similar flow when

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.970
it's a multiplayer experience.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:23.490
So a gamer starts by
logging into their profile,

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.100
loading up their attributes,
their skill level,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.440
other items that will be
used later on in their play

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.520
experience.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:30.810
That's step one.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.440
That's a platform
service API call.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.450
Step two, once I'm
signed in, I need

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.760
to find some friends
to play a game with.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.500
That's called matchmaking.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.690
So I make a call to
a matchmaker service.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.900
I get assigned to a match
with a bunch of my friends.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.670
And next up, we
need to play a game.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.710
We need to get
assigned into a match.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.398
So the third step
in the process is

00:05:52.398 --> 00:05:54.690
that this group of people
who've been assigned together

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:59.120
into a match get assigned
to a game server.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.810
And then finally, as
we're interacting together

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.150
in that game, lots
of interesting events

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:05.690
are being generated.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.620
So we want to capture those
events in a data warehouse,

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.330
so that you can analyze
what your players are doing,

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:15.220
think about how to improve
the customer experience,

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.580
and how to monetize your
game most effectively.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.790
So in today's talk, we're
going to focus mostly

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:25.000
on this part of the
equation-- the game servers.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.940
So let's start by defining
what game servers are so

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.490
that we have a common
language for discussion.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.120
So what are game servers?

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:43.630
And why do I need them
for my multiplayer game?

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.400
So we'll start with
the definition,

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.420
and then we'll dig in
a little bit deeper.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.840
But a game server is
the authoritative source

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.870
of events for your game.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:57.280
A game server broadcasts that
state to all of the clients

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.820
so that they can visualize an
accurate representation of what

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.070
that player should be seeing.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.410
The game server also
captures and processes

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.530
all of the inputs
from your player.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.030
And the game server
needs to do this

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:13.300
on a very consistent and
timely manner in order

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.855
to provide a smooth and fluid
experience to your players.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:23.700
So let's use an example,
one of my favorite games,

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.380
Apex Legends.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.500
Who loves Apex Legends?

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:29.430
Awesome.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.680
Let's use Apex
Legends as an example.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:34.440
So to understand what
a game server does,

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.710
we need to also understand
what a game client does,

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.180
and how the client
and the server

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.110
interact with one another.

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:44.430
So on the left hand side,
you can see a picture

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.380
of what a game client does.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.740
So let's take a match
of Apex Legends.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.040
60 players, battle
royale format.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.580
You have 60 game clients,
one for each player.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.640
They're going to be running
on a PC, or a console,

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.630
or for some games, they might
be running on a mobile device.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:05.690
The responsibility
of a game client

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.810
is to render the graphical
experience that that player is

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:09.950
experiencing.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:13.760
Graphics, sound, and input are
the primary responsibilities

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.690
of the client.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.750
Because this is a graphically
intensive workload,

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:21.840
this is where your
GPU comes into play.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.310
So a game client is
heavily reliant on a GPU

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.040
to get its job done.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:30.480
So if graphics are what
a game client focuses on

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.720
for one player, then what
does a game server do?

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.010
Well game server is
running in a data center

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.770
on a host or a virtual machine.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.659
That's the first difference.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:45.300
All of the players that got
assigned into that match--

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:46.830
all 60 players--

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:52.070
their game clients are going
to connect to that game server.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:55.100
The game server, contrary
to what you might believe,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.690
doesn't process graphics at all.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.810
The game server is responsible
for the state of that game

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:01.760
world.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.710
And because the game
server is dealing

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:06.290
with data rather
than graphics, this

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:08.870
is a CPU intensive process.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:11.700
You don't typically have a
GPU involved in a game server

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:12.200
at all.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:18.050
So an analogy that I
like to use is, a game

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:21.590
server for this island
that 60 players are

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.170
dropping onto,
the game server is

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:27.050
sort of penciling in the
details of that world,

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.970
and drawing out a blueprint.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:30.470
And then that
blueprint is sent out

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:34.940
to the game clients where a high
fidelity graphic representation

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.130
of what that player
sees is rendered,

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:40.280
based on that blueprint that's
generated by the server.

00:09:43.677 --> 00:09:45.510
So let's talk a little
bit more specifically

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.310
about what game servers do.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.460
Game servers have to receive
the input from the players.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.290
Based on that
input, game servers

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:56.910
are going to track the positions
of those players on the map.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.090
Those game players are going
to interact with obstacles.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:01.590
They're going to
run into buildings.

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:03.570
They might even run
into each other.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:05.970
The game server is going
to process what's happening

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.230
when those objects collide.

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:11.680
We have physics like gravity.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.950
In combat games, you're going
to have particles flying around.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:17.920
So the game server is
calculating velocities and hits

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:19.510
or misses.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.700
And so once all those
calculations are performed,

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.610
the game server is broadcasting
the results back out

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:27.740
to those game clients so that
they can render the results.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:31.510
And this has to happen very
consistently, up to 120 times

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:33.700
a second in very
high fidelity games.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.330
So let's go back to our
example of Apex Legends.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.250
And let's talk through
what's actually

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.250
happening as we're playing
through that game of Apex.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:47.590
So these battle royale games
all start in about the same way.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:50.880
You've got a big island, an
airship passes over the island,

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.210
and those 60 players
begin to skydive one

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:59.010
by one onto that island, falling
at terminal velocity, 160

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:03.180
miles an hour to their preferred
destinations on that map.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:06.000
The game server is
calculating the positions

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.130
of all those players as
they're falling to the ground.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.670
Now those players are
adjusting their body position,

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.130
which changes wind
resistance, velocity,

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.470
trajectory, acceleration.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.350
All of these things are being
recalculated in real time

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.810
by that game server.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.940
So next, those players
land on the island.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.680
They begin picking up loot.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.650
They begin picking up
items, weapons, preparing

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.360
for the battle ahead.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.690
The game server is keeping track
of each player's inventory,

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:36.750
and the position of
objects on the island.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:40.290
Players start going upstairs,
entering buildings, running

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.490
into walls.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:44.497
Now we're doing
collision detection.

00:11:44.497 --> 00:11:46.080
And once all the
players have equipped

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:49.290
themselves sufficiently,
the combat begins.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.570
Projectiles start flying around.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.760
Hits and misses are calculated.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.620
If we have explosive
projectiles,

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.170
we have particles that
are being generated.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:01.733
All of this is being
calculated by the game server.

00:12:01.733 --> 00:12:03.150
And at the beginning
of the match,

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.070
we start with 60 players.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:09.440
And as the match goes on,
we get down to one player--

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.020
the ultimate victor in the game.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.270
And so what you might realize
is that at the beginning

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.250
of the match, there's
a whole lot of work

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:19.820
that game server has to do
to calculate all 60 players'

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:22.820
inputs and positions.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.370
At the end of the game,
there's a lot less work to do.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:28.040
But regardless of that
changing workload,

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.290
the game server has to
maintain a constant tick

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:31.995
rate throughout the match.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:38.690
So you might say, OK, I've
been playing multiplayer games

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.265
for a while.

00:12:40.265 --> 00:12:41.640
What do we need
game servers for?

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:45.670
We used to have this
thing called peer-to-peer.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:48.070
And a peer-to-peer
model, let's say

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:50.380
we've got a few PCs that
have been assigned to a match

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:52.840
together, and we just
elect one of them

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:55.900
to also take on the
role of the game server.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.610
So that PC will become
not only the game client,

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:01.560
but also the game server.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.960
Well, so there are a few
problems with this approach.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:09.970
Number one, your PCs are
located on a home network,

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:12.910
and there's lots of variables
out in that environment.

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.060
How do we pick which
one of these PCs

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.410
should become the server?

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:18.660
Should we pick the
highest clock speed?

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:22.680
Should we pick the lowest
latency or the best bandwidth?

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.980
Let's say that we go with
the best network connection.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.020
Now after the match
starts, let's say

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.985
someone else in that house
starts streaming a 4K video.

00:13:30.985 --> 00:13:32.610
Or let's say that
someone in that house

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:36.210
starts downloading a huge file
on a peer-to-peer network.

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:39.090
All of a sudden, the network
characteristics of that PC

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:41.870
have fundamentally changed.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:43.650
Let's talk about scale.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:49.370
Today's battle royale games
have 50, 60, 100 players.

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:54.290
A few network connections
into a consumer network,

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:55.860
that scales just fine.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:59.480
Let's try 100 connections
into a PC on a home network.

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:01.810
It doesn't scale.

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.960
And finally, and probably
the most significant issue

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:06.880
is that-- remember,
we said that server

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.940
needs to be authoritative.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:13.690
So if that binary is
deployed onto someone's PC,

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.820
they can hack that binary.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.280
They can mess with the
authoritative nature

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.020
of that game server.

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.260
All of the traffic from
all of those players

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.520
is coming into that
person's home network.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.250
They can interfere
with that traffic

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:31.750
to change the balance of
the game in their own favor.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.090
So peer-to-peer was great
when we got started.

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:36.630
But for the types of high
fidelity experiences people

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:40.600
want today, we've
outgrown peer-to-peer.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.785
So how are dedicated
servers different?

00:14:42.785 --> 00:14:44.610
We've got one
additional component

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:48.030
that we bring into play, which
is that we deploy that game

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:53.270
server into a virtual machine
in a professional data center.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:55.160
What does this do for us?

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:58.380
It brings us
additional consistency.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:01.310
Now my network is being
managed by professionals.

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:03.845
It's being monitored by SREs.

00:15:03.845 --> 00:15:06.470
I don't have the kinds of issues
that I have on a home network.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.580
I have high speed
network interconnects

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:14.040
on these virtual machines,
so I can scale up

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:17.940
to more connections
per game server.

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:22.090
And most important of all,
that binary is firewalled.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:24.460
In a professional
environment, it's much harder

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.550
to interfere with that binary
and with the network traffic

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.510
going into that data center.

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:33.880
So now we're ready for a world
class multiplayer experience.

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:40.810
OK, so we understand
what game servers are

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:45.220
and why we need them to deliver
world class experiences.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:46.810
What matters for game servers?

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.600
We need to understand
this to be able to deliver

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:54.120
the type of services that
high fidelity games demand.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.280
Performance matters.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.970
And there's two key
metrics for a game server.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.950
The first is latency.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:05.410
This is a network metric.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:08.490
So if we have game clients
that are communicating

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:11.280
multiple times per
second with that server

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:15.180
and need to receive replies
back multiple times per second,

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:18.060
we need the lowest
possible network latency

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:21.790
between those clients
and that server.

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:23.400
So that's our network benchmark.

00:16:25.817 --> 00:16:27.650
And then if we're running
on our game server

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:31.340
in a tight loop that's
processing all of those events

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:35.620
that I told you about for up
to 100 players in a match,

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:42.010
our CPU needs to be able to
process that loop consistently.

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:43.320
So we call that frame time.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:45.547
So that's our CPU
performance metric.

00:16:45.547 --> 00:16:47.880
We're going to dig into both
of these a little bit more,

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:51.160
because these are
really important.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:52.170
So let's talk latency.

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:56.100
What kind of latency do we
need for a real time game?

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:00.630
Generally speaking, for a real
time game like an Apex Legends,

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.600
you're going to want about 25
milliseconds round trip time

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.550
to your clients or less.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.577
That's preferred.

00:17:07.577 --> 00:17:10.160
Some games you might be able to
get away with higher latency--

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:13.310
50 milliseconds, maybe
100 milliseconds.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:15.210
It's going to
depend on the game.

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:17.569
But for the types of
games we're talking about,

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:20.060
25 milliseconds or
less is preferred.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:25.750
So let's look at a map
of the United States.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:29.050
Let's say that we're running
our game server on one coast,

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:32.140
and we're running a
client on another coast.

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:34.960
You've got at least 50
milliseconds, typically,

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:37.460
between those two endpoints.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:40.690
So we've already doubled
our latency goal.

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:43.420
That's not going to work.

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:47.150
Well if I'm looking at this
map, and I'm familiar with GCP,

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.150
I might say, you know,
those little blue flags--

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:50.878
those are Google data centers.

00:17:50.878 --> 00:17:52.420
I'm going to be
clever, and I'm going

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:56.270
to put all my game servers in
that one right in the middle.

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:57.950
Well, networks are funny things.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.920
You might expect the
latency to cut in half.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.470
But it's not always a
function just of distance.

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:06.800
It can be
carrier-based, and there

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:12.560
can be other concerns like the
number of hops between the two

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:14.210
endpoints.

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:18.290
So deploying all of your game
servers on a continental basis,

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:21.142
generally, is not
going to hit the mark.

00:18:21.142 --> 00:18:22.600
So you're typically
going to end up

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:25.180
with something that
looks like this, where

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:27.520
you deploy collections
of game servers

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:30.200
to several different
data centers,

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.520
and then you assign players
in a radius around those data

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:38.000
centers to the closest
possible deployment.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:40.310
There's a bit of an art to this.

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.830
Because as you
shrink those circles,

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:45.020
you start decreasing
the number of people

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:47.272
that can play a game together.

00:18:47.272 --> 00:18:48.980
So if you shrink those
circles too small,

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:51.530
your matchmaking pool
becomes too small,

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:54.410
and people can't find
other players to play with.

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:57.780
But if it gets too big,
your latency is too high.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:01.130
So there's a bit of an art to
balancing those two things.

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:03.740
And helping you figure out
how to deploy your game

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.990
servers based on your
audience is something

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:06.950
that we can help with.

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:11.460
So that's latency.

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:14.450
Now let's talk about our
CPU benchmark-- frame rates

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:15.840
and budgets.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.260
Now again, imagine
in a simplified model

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:19.910
of a game server
that you just have

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:21.845
a while loop that's running.

00:19:21.845 --> 00:19:23.720
And all of those things
that I told you about

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.860
are a line item in
that while loop.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:29.540
You're going to want to go
through each tick of that while

00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:33.510
loop in exactly the same amount
of time every single frame.

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:35.450
So we call it a tick rate.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:38.180
Sometimes it's also
called a frame rate.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:41.690
And so for a battle royale
game, a typical tick rate

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:44.420
might be 20 to 30
ticks per second--

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:46.280
20 to 30 Hz.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:48.530
So that's exactly the
frequency that you want

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.880
to iterate through that loop.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:54.140
So if you have a
tick rate of 20 Hz,

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:56.330
you've got about 50
milliseconds to make it

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:59.560
through a single pass of
that game server's main loop.

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:05.617
50 milliseconds may
sound like a lot of time.

00:20:05.617 --> 00:20:07.700
But let's take into
consideration how many players

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:08.700
we've got in that match.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.010
We have 60 players.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:12.740
So per player, I
have less than one

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:16.960
millisecond to get
through that main loop.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:20.900
One to two milliseconds is
a big deal on a game server.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:23.685
So there's not a lot
of room for variance.

00:20:23.685 --> 00:20:25.810
And we'll talk more about
variance in a little bit.

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:32.160
So let's zoom in on what's
happening during those frames.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:35.160
This is two frames being
processed on a game server.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.530
We're going with
that 20 Hz tick rate.

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:40.988
We have 50 milliseconds to
get through a single frame.

00:20:40.988 --> 00:20:42.780
In this example, we're
actually able to get

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.568
all of our work done
in 40 milliseconds.

00:20:45.568 --> 00:20:46.110
That's great.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:47.550
We got done early.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:50.400
But remember, we have to
wake up for the next frame

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.170
right on the boundary
of 50 milliseconds.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:56.170
So we're going to go to
sleep for 10 milliseconds.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:59.100
Then we wake up and start
processing the next frame.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:02.580
This is what we want to see
frame after frame after frame.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.250
We finish within budget,
and maybe a little bit

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:08.920
leftover to account
for variance.

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:12.070
This is what we
don't want to see.

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:15.600
So in this example, still
50 millisecond frame budget,

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:18.930
it took me 60 milliseconds
to process that frame.

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:21.240
Now I'm into the next frame.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.960
What am I going to do here?

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:26.600
I'm going to have to
drop that second frame.

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:28.130
So all of my clients
are not going

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:31.738
to receive an update
for that frame.

00:21:31.738 --> 00:21:33.905
So let's think about what
this looks like over time.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:39.800
The top scenario is
what we want to see.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:43.030
We want to see every single one
of those frames consistently

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:44.440
processed.

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.990
Because when this happens,
all of our players

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:52.040
are going to have a very fluid,
very consistent experience.

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.003
That game is going to
flow just like a movie

00:21:54.003 --> 00:21:54.920
that they're watching.

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:56.940
That's what we want.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:59.760
In the bottom example,
we're dropping frames

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:02.350
pretty frequently.

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.170
What that's going to result in
is what we call rubber banding,

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:06.310
stuttering.

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:08.950
You're going to see
skipping around in the game.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:11.080
And after maybe a
few minutes of that,

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.480
you're going to have some
very unhappy players that are

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.580
going to start logging tickets.

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:17.470
Not good.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:19.120
So let's be a little
bit more specific.

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:21.700
What do we mean when
we say "consistent?"

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:24.940
For most games, we want
to process those frames

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:30.110
within budget, at least 95%
of the time, if not higher.

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:32.770
So we need a very, very
high level of consistency

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:34.660
and a very low
level of variance.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:41.600
So one of the
things that I'm very

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:47.510
excited to talk about today
is our compute platform.

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:50.810
Clouds are busy places.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:55.100
Running a game in the cloud
is very different than running

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:57.500
a game on a bare metal server.

00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:59.300
We have a hypervisor
on that cloud,

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:04.400
scheduling resources that has
overhead associated with it.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:07.850
And you have other tenants
that are running processes

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:09.830
on that same host.

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:12.650
They may be running
game servers also.

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:16.640
Those two things can
introduce significant variance

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:22.430
into the performance that you're
encountering on a game server.

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:26.640
So up until today, we've had
one type of virtual machine.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.180
If you've used GCP, you're
very familiar with N1

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.310
standard virtual machines.

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:32.730
They're really cool.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.872
We abstract all of the
underlying hardware away.

00:23:35.872 --> 00:23:37.580
And what that means
is, I can go in there

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:42.780
and dial in exactly how many
VCPUs use I want from 1 to 96.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:45.800
I can dial in exactly
how much RAM I want.

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:48.920
Very customizable,
very flexible.

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:51.822
But in order to accomplish
that, very abstracted

00:23:51.822 --> 00:23:53.030
from the underlying hardware.

00:23:55.620 --> 00:23:58.740
Also, our N1
standards on Skylake,

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:04.860
which is our newest generation
of hardware up until today,

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.020
runs at 2.7 GHz.

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:11.820
That's our sustained clock
speed across all cores.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.060
So today, we're introducing an
entirely new hardware platform

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:17.880
called Compute Optimized VMs.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:19.980
And these are going to
bump the clock speed

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:22.590
up significantly to 3.8 GHz.

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:25.772
And that's sustained
across all cores.

00:24:25.772 --> 00:24:27.480
And we've also got
some features in there

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.522
that are going to reduce
the variance that you're

00:24:29.522 --> 00:24:32.320
going to see even at
that high clock speed.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:34.470
So let's dig in a little bit.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:38.220
So compared to our
Skylakes running on N1s,

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.550
you're going to see a 40%
increase in performance

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:44.190
on our Compute Optimized VMs.

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:45.330
What does that mean?

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:48.510
Conceptually, that processing
time in your main loop

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:50.790
decreased by 40%.

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:54.880
You get a lot more
room for error.

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:57.210
You can also do a lot
more within that same 50

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:58.980
milliseconds.

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:01.530
We've also increased
the speed of the network

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:04.240
up to 32 gigabits per second.

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:07.590
Now I can think about connecting
more players to my game server

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.150
because I've got
more clock cycles,

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:11.085
and I've got more
throughput on the network.

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:15.830
So that's high performance.

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:18.400
Let's talk about variance.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:21.130
So I've illustrated
a physical host here.

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:24.160
This is a conceptual version
of what a host in the Cloud

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:25.360
looks like.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:29.710
We have very large CPUs,
typically more than one socket

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:31.830
running on a host.

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.330
Those sockets are connected
with an interconnect,

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:38.670
and they each have
a local RAM bus.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:41.890
In an N1 standard, that's
all abstracted away from you.

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:47.110
You just have a big bucket of
cores and a big bucket of RAM.

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:52.210
Let's say that you're running
a game on the CPU on the left,

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:55.600
and some of your RAM has been
scheduled onto the RAM bank

00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:57.610
for the other socket.

00:25:57.610 --> 00:25:59.770
I'm going to have to reach
across that interconnect

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:01.750
to access my RAM,
which is going to be

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:06.620
a lot slower than if I were to
access that same RAM locally.

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:08.590
And I don't have any
visibility or control

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:11.250
into that on a standard VM.

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:14.500
In a lot of cases, that's
still fine for your game.

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:17.410
But for cases that are very
sensitive to performance,

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:19.750
with our Compute
Optimized VMs, we're

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:22.030
exposing the underlying
hardware platform,

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:23.920
which is Cascade Lake.

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:25.960
So now you'll be able
to have transparency

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:29.230
into the physical hardware
that's running on the host.

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:33.620
And we're also
exposing this topology.

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:37.790
So now you have visibility into
the NUMA topology of the host.

00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:40.240
So it gives you a
lot more control

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:42.560
over the workload
that you're running.

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:44.890
We also do some cool things
like pinning your RAM

00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:46.060
to the local socket.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:48.190
So if you run the
right size shape,

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:50.860
you can effectively
capture that socket,

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:54.580
and your RAM will be pinned
to that local RAM node.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:56.440
So some really cool
stuff for gaming

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:58.240
with Compute Optimized VMs.

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:00.580
Those go into alpha today.

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:03.550
I've got my contact
information on the last slide.

00:27:03.550 --> 00:27:05.895
And if you're interested
in starting to test them,

00:27:05.895 --> 00:27:08.020
reach out to me, and I'll
help you get that set up.

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:13.720
All right, let's talk
about scaling up.

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:16.373
So now that we know it's
needed to run a game server,

00:27:16.373 --> 00:27:18.165
we want to run a whole
lot of game servers.

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:23.450
So how do we scale a game
server up for global audiences?

00:27:28.485 --> 00:27:30.110
So in the picture of
the United States,

00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:33.470
you saw a few
different GCP regions.

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:38.670
Globally, we have 19 regions.

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:40.040
We have four more coming.

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.340
That'll be 23 regions.

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:45.270
So those are different
locations that you

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:48.150
can place your virtual
machines as close as

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:52.820
possible to the gamers that
will be connecting to them.

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:55.600
So this picture might look
like a lot like the picture

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:57.850
that you see from other clouds.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.770
All clouds have regions.

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:01.990
We all have zones.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:04.480
One of the fundamental
differences between our Cloud

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:07.120
and other clouds is
that all of our regions

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:09.670
and all of our edge pops
are connected together

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:13.470
with our private fiber
optic interconnect.

00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:16.410
So in other clouds, when you're
transiting between data centers

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:18.110
or between edge
pops, you're going

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:20.650
to be transiting the internet.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:24.840
So you're subject to the network
topology of the internet.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.440
With Google Cloud, when you're
transiting between data centers

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:33.440
and from pop to data center,
it's all on our private fiber.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:36.680
So I mentioned, we
have 19 regions.

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:39.740
That's a pretty nice
selection of locations

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:41.540
to deploy your servers.

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:43.490
If you look a little
closer on this diagram,

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:45.560
there's all these gray dots.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:48.240
Those are our edge
pop locations.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:53.270
We have 134 edge pop locations,
all with fiber interconnects

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:56.850
back to our data centers.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:59.542
Game servers use public IPs.

00:28:59.542 --> 00:29:01.500
That's how our game
clients out on the internet

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:02.970
connect to them.

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:05.940
When I spin up a
virtual machine in GCP,

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:10.320
that public IP is globally
advertised at all 134

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.390
of those edge pops.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:15.000
So when your client goes to
connect to the region that's

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.280
closest to him,
he's actually going

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:20.220
to connect to the edge
pop that's even closer.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:23.778
And then he's going to transit
the fiber into our data center.

00:29:23.778 --> 00:29:25.320
So this is how we're
going to get you

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:27.690
as close as possible
to your gamers,

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:30.930
and give you the lowest possible
latency into those locations.

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:37.050
OK, so let's talk about scaling.

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:40.230
Now that we understand we have
a global private fiber optic

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:44.385
network to run our
games on only with GCP,

00:29:44.385 --> 00:29:45.760
let's think about
how we're going

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:48.620
to scale up on that network.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:52.190
And we've got three
different options.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:54.820
I mentioned earlier that
Google is all about open.

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:56.333
We love choice.

00:29:56.333 --> 00:29:58.750
So we have three different
ways that you can run your game

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:01.310
servers in our Cloud.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:03.880
You can build the scaling
infrastructure yourself,

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:06.970
and some of you have already.

00:30:06.970 --> 00:30:09.940
We have an awesome
managed partner

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:14.040
that you can work with to help
you run your game servers.

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:17.390
And you can look into using
Kubernetes and containers,

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:20.730
and leveraging the power
of Kubernetes to scale.

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:23.430
Let's talk about
these three options.

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:26.300
So let's start with
building it ourselves.

00:30:28.745 --> 00:30:30.870
So let's say that we want
to deploy our game server

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:33.770
into three different zones.

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:36.170
We're going to use the
basic fundamental building

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:37.880
blocks of Google Cloud.

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:39.350
We're going to use
virtual machines

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:41.570
and the APIs
associated with them.

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:45.000
And to scale the collection of
virtual machines up and down,

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.240
we're going to use managed
instance groups, which

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:51.980
will create new virtual
machines for us very easily.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:54.370
So in each zone, we'll
have a collection of VMs,

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:58.900
and a managed instance
group to scale up and down.

00:30:58.900 --> 00:31:01.668
So that's the GCP infrastructure
that we're going to use.

00:31:01.668 --> 00:31:03.960
We're also going to need some
additional infrastructure

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:05.377
that we're going
to have to build.

00:31:05.377 --> 00:31:08.430
It's typically called a server
manager or an autoscaler.

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:10.080
And that piece of
software is going

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:12.480
to be responsible
for calling out

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:15.360
to the appropriate managed
instance groups and VMs

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:19.100
and scaling them up and down.

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:20.970
Well that sounds pretty easy.

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:23.190
I don't have to
depend on anyone else.

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:25.890
This actually turns out to
be a pretty complex piece

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:27.440
of software to write.

00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:29.520
And in order to understand
why it's complex,

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:32.392
we need to look at how
game servers are deployed.

00:31:32.392 --> 00:31:33.850
But generally
speaking, we're going

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:37.050
to recommend this option for
those that have already written

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:41.400
this piece of software for their
own bare metal environments,

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:43.530
for another cloud,
and simply would

00:31:43.530 --> 00:31:46.500
need to retrofit it to
use our managed instance

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:49.045
groups and VMs.

00:31:49.045 --> 00:31:50.670
So let's look at what
game servers look

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:52.680
like when they're deployed.

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:54.450
Do we just spin up
a whole bunch of VMs

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:56.310
for each one of
these game servers?

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:57.240
Typically not.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:00.250
A game server is a pretty
lightweight process.

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:02.190
It's typically single threaded.

00:32:02.190 --> 00:32:04.020
For that reason, it
typically only needs

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:07.140
a single core, maybe two cores.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:09.120
And so there's too much
overhead in spinning up

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:12.270
a virtual machine for one VCPU.

00:32:12.270 --> 00:32:15.150
So what's commonly done,
even on bare metal,

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:17.440
is you bring up a larger
host, and then you

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:20.910
bin pack a bunch of
processes onto that host,

00:32:20.910 --> 00:32:23.820
utilizing however many
cores your particular game

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:25.360
server needs.

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:30.210
So in this example, I've got
an N1 standard 16, 16 VCPUs,

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:33.780
and I've got about 16 game
server processes running

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:36.830
on that single virtual machine.

00:32:36.830 --> 00:32:39.520
So when you're thinking about
scaling up and scaling down,

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.800
you're not only spinning
up virtual machines,

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:44.170
but you have to worry
about all that bin packing.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:47.110
You have to think about how
to drain that host as matches

00:32:47.110 --> 00:32:48.670
come to a close.

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:50.800
So there's a lot
of lifecycle that

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:53.410
needs to be built into
your server manager, not

00:32:53.410 --> 00:32:54.505
to mention error handling.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:59.700
But for shops that have
built their own logic,

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:01.410
this can be a great route to go.

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:05.280
We're actually very excited
that the Division 2 is running

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:07.590
with this methodology on GCP.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:10.560
So that game launched
on GCP very recently.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:14.280
We're very excited that they're
running at scale in our Cloud

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:16.260
using this methodology.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:19.200
And what Massive really loved
about working with Google

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:20.550
is that we understand games.

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:22.020
We're gamers.

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:26.010
We love digging in and helping
companies scale their games up

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:26.670
in our Cloud.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.710
So that's the build
it yourself model.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:34.080
And you can scale
games way up if you're

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:36.610
willing to invest in
that infrastructure.

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:42.630
Let's talk about the
managed partner option.

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:45.120
Maybe you don't want
to invest all that time

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:47.640
in building your own
scaling infrastructure.

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:49.650
And instead, you'd
prefer to enlist

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:54.210
the expertise of a partner who's
a professional, running game

00:33:54.210 --> 00:34:01.110
servers, who can provide
24/7 365 service, who

00:34:01.110 --> 00:34:05.460
can run your game servers,
not only in Cloud, but also

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:12.030
in a collection of bare metal
colos located globally--

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:14.340
a partner who has 20
years of experience

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:17.260
running game servers.

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:21.280
We're very excited to work
with Multiplay as our game

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:23.170
server hosting partner.

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:25.239
And they can provide
a managed partner

00:34:25.239 --> 00:34:27.489
level of service to you.

00:34:27.489 --> 00:34:31.870
And we're even more excited to
talk about Apex Legends, which

00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:35.710
uses the multiplayer
service on GCP to scale up

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:38.090
for a global audience.

00:34:38.090 --> 00:34:40.610
Some of these numbers
are pretty incredible.

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:47.630
So Apex Legends scaled up to a
million players in eight hours.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:49.659
Think about that for a minute.

00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:51.328
That number seems incredible.

00:34:51.328 --> 00:34:52.870
But then think about
the fact that it

00:34:52.870 --> 00:34:57.390
scaled up to 10 million
players in three days,

00:34:57.390 --> 00:35:00.360
and then multiplied
that number by five

00:35:00.360 --> 00:35:05.020
and scaled up to 50 million
players in only four weeks.

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:07.350
This is exactly
the kind of outcome

00:35:07.350 --> 00:35:09.300
that you want for your game.

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:12.150
And this is why you would work
with a partner like Multiplay,

00:35:12.150 --> 00:35:17.860
and run your game on a
global cloud, like GCP.

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:21.340
If you read any of the press
around the Apex Legends launch,

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:24.790
it was really positive about
how smooth the experience was

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:25.960
for the players.

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:30.490
This game just went from zero
to millions without a hitch.

00:35:30.490 --> 00:35:32.680
And that's because of that
combination of Multiplay

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:34.450
and GCP scalability.

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:37.160
This is a very
exciting use case.

00:35:37.160 --> 00:35:39.970
So then let's talk about the
third option for scaling game

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:41.740
servers.

00:35:41.740 --> 00:35:47.340
The third option is leveraging
the power of Kubernetes.

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:51.000
So when you think back to that
diagram that I drew earlier,

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:54.450
a virtual machine with
a bunch of processes bin

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:56.190
packed onto it.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:58.890
That sounds an awful lot
like a Kubernetes node

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:01.920
with a bunch of containers
bin packed onto it.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:06.770
Kubernetes is really,
really good at doing that.

00:36:06.770 --> 00:36:10.840
However, Kubernetes is doesn't
understand game servers.

00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:14.060
Kubernetes is designed
for stateless workloads.

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:18.000
It's designed for
HTTP workloads.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:20.320
So we built an
extension to Kubernetes

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:24.380
is called Agones with
Ubisoft that teaches

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:27.020
Kubernetes about game servers.

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:30.700
It teaches Kubernetes is
how to handle UDP endpoints.

00:36:30.700 --> 00:36:32.960
It teaches Kubernetes
about game server

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:35.600
lifecycle so that
Kubernetes is doesn't

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:37.700
decide to scale down
your game servers

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:40.330
in the middle of a match.

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:44.140
So with Agones, we're
building an open API

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:47.070
to run and scale your
games with Kubernetes

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:50.630
in an environment
of your choosing.

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:54.770
And in the same way that we
approached Kubernetes and GKE,

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:57.530
we're working on a
managed service for Agones

00:36:57.530 --> 00:36:59.630
as well that we'd love
to talk to you about.

00:37:02.330 --> 00:37:05.860
So we're excited that
Supersolid has chosen to launch

00:37:05.860 --> 00:37:07.840
their new game, Snake Rivals.

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:11.440
This will be the first
production game on Agones.

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:15.310
And we're just thrilled to have
this game launching on Agones.

00:37:15.310 --> 00:37:20.080
We're working with many other
studios on non-production games

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:21.820
for Agones.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:24.220
So you have these three
different choices,

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:25.600
and you can choose
which of these

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:28.995
is the best fit for your
team and for your game.

00:37:28.995 --> 00:37:30.370
And in any of
these three, you're

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:33.028
going to know that you've
got Google's infrastructure

00:37:33.028 --> 00:37:34.570
powering your game
behind the scenes.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:42.353
So let's talk about the
future of cloud gaming--

00:37:42.353 --> 00:37:43.395
this thing called Stadia.

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:49.840
So our client and
server discussion

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:52.460
will come in handy here.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:57.530
Stadia is a new kind
of gaming platform.

00:37:57.530 --> 00:38:01.040
It brings high
fidelity AAA games

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:02.930
to any screen that
can run Chrome.

00:38:06.230 --> 00:38:11.300
It does that by moving that
game client that we talked about

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:14.720
earlier with its
GPU into the Cloud,

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:18.110
rendering the content
in a Cloud Data Center,

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:20.390
and then streaming
that content as if it

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:23.345
was a video to a Chrome screen.

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.780
So in a sense, if we've moved
the game server into the Cloud

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:33.680
with GCP, we've moved the
game client into the Cloud

00:38:33.680 --> 00:38:36.830
with Stadia.

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:40.160
So let's talk about what that
private fiber optic network

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:41.435
does for us with Stadia.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:47.040
10 to 25 milliseconds
to our clients

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:51.050
sounded pretty good
without Stadia.

00:38:51.050 --> 00:38:55.940
Now our clients are running on
the same fiber optic network

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:59.390
that our servers are running on.

00:38:59.390 --> 00:39:02.730
So think about what that
does to your average latency

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:07.580
to your clients, and what kinds
of possibilities that opens up

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:10.700
for new types of games, and
new types of interactions that

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:12.890
haven't been possible
when your game's clients

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:15.750
were distributed
on the internet.

00:39:15.750 --> 00:39:18.210
Now we mentioned
that Google is open.

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:21.150
You can still run your
game servers in a colo

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:24.292
or in another cloud, and
it'll work with Stadia.

00:39:24.292 --> 00:39:26.250
But you're going to be
back to that old latency

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:29.850
model, where you're transiting
the internet 10 to 25

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:31.810
milliseconds.

00:39:31.810 --> 00:39:34.020
So we're really excited
about the new types of games

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:37.320
that people will build when the
entire game infrastructure is

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:39.375
on the same private
fiber optic network.

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:44.290
So here is a bit more
of a detailed view

00:39:44.290 --> 00:39:47.560
of what the architecture
would look like.

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:49.560
So the only thing that's
out on the internet now

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:52.000
is your Chrome device.

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:54.070
Your game client has
been moved into one

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:58.420
of those edge pops that I showed
earlier in our network diagram.

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:00.340
So it's even closer
to your players

00:40:00.340 --> 00:40:02.158
than the game server is.

00:40:02.158 --> 00:40:03.700
But it's communicating
with your game

00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:09.140
servers over that private
fiber that you saw earlier.

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:10.172
Here's the same picture.

00:40:10.172 --> 00:40:12.380
If you're running your game
servers and another cloud

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:15.860
or in a colo, you're still
going to get great performance,

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:18.380
but you're not going to get
that same level of low latency

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:19.490
that you're going to
get with everything

00:40:19.490 --> 00:40:20.657
running on the same network.

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:27.790
So what do Stadia
and GCP do together?

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:28.930
Security.

00:40:28.930 --> 00:40:31.600
All of your network traffic
now is on a private fiber

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:35.140
optic network, except for
the streaming data going back

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:36.280
to your clients.

00:40:36.280 --> 00:40:39.040
Performance we've
already talked about.

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:41.670
Let's talk a little
bit about egress.

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:44.130
One of the significant costs
of running game servers

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:45.420
can be egress.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:48.660
If I'm sending updates to
hundreds of players tens

00:40:48.660 --> 00:40:52.920
or hundreds of times a second,
that generates a lot of egress.

00:40:52.920 --> 00:40:55.200
You have to pay for
egress in the Cloud.

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:59.880
When your egress is
going from GCP to Stadia,

00:40:59.880 --> 00:41:03.910
we've worked out a significant
discount on the egress.

00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:05.780
So it'll be actually
less expensive

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:08.360
to run your game servers
on GCP if they're

00:41:08.360 --> 00:41:11.230
interacting with Stadia.

00:41:11.230 --> 00:41:13.810
So we're really excited
about this combination.

00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:16.240
We're excited about
the new types of games

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:17.590
that you'll build.

00:41:17.590 --> 00:41:20.080
And we're very excited
to work with you to scale

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:24.010
those games up in the Cloud.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.170
So we'd love your
feedback on the session.

00:41:27.170 --> 00:41:31.040
My contact information is
at the bottom of the screen.

00:41:31.040 --> 00:41:33.550
Feel free to email
me or reach out

00:41:33.550 --> 00:41:35.320
to me on Twitter or LinkedIn.

00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:38.650
If you're interested in
those Compute Optimized VMs,

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:39.280
let me know.

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:41.167
I'll help you get whitelisted.

00:41:41.167 --> 00:41:43.000
We think they're going
to be pretty awesome.

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:46.630
We've gotten some early feedback
from a major AAA studio.

00:41:46.630 --> 00:41:48.850
And they're very, very
